                {n.s.map((s, k) => <text x={n.x + n.w / 2} y={n.y + (n.s.length > 1 ? 62 : 70) + k * 24} text-anchor="middle" class="sub">{s}</text>)}
              </g>
            ))}
          </svg>
          <p class="foot rv" data-at="4">Browsers read cached frames and results. More viewers add no inference workers.</p>
          <aside class="notes" hidden>
            <p>Build left to right. Sources, one scheduler, two AI branches (vehicles; plates), the tracker and gates, SQLite with WAL and model hashes, then FastAPI, the React dashboard and signed reports.</p>
            <p>The amber packets are the plate branch. The design choice: inference is owned by one process, so viewers never add load.</p>
          </aside>
        </section>

        {hasClip("manim-pipeline") && (
          <section class="slide s-clip" data-builds="0" data-t="fade" aria-label="Pipeline animation">
            <header class="hd"><p class="kicker">The pipeline, animated</p><span class="tag i">Animation</span></header>
            <div class="clipbox"><Clip name="manim-pipeline" label="Animated walk through the Sakshi pipeline" /></div>
            <aside class="notes" hidden><p>Let the animation run. Narrate: frame sampled, detected, plate read, gated, stored, served.</p></aside>
          </section>
        )}

        <!-- Scheduler -->
        <section class="slide s-two wide" data-builds="2" data-t="slide" aria-label="Shared scheduler">
          <div>
            <header class="hd"><p class="kicker">Scheduling</p></header>
            <h2>Every camera shares one frame budget</h2>
            <ul class="plain">
              <li>A configurable <b>global</b> budget (default 2 frames per second), not per camera.</li>
              <li>Round-robin over available sources, so no camera starves.</li>
              <li class="rv" data-at="1">Missing or retrying sources do not burn inference slots.</li>
              <li class="rv" data-at="2">To grow: partition cameras across workers. Viewers read the cache and add no load.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="media">
            <Clip name="manim-scheduler" label="Animation of the round-robin scheduler">
              <svg class="ringsvg" viewBox="0 0 460 460" aria-hidden="true">
                <circle cx="230" cy="220" r="168" class="ring__track" />
                <g class="hand"><path d="M230 220 L230 62" /><circle cx="230" cy="62" r="7" /></g>
                {ring.map((r) => (
                  <g class="rnode" style={`--d:${r.k * 0.5}s`}>
                    <circle cx={r.x} cy={r.y} r="30" />
                    <text x={r.x} y={r.y + 6} text-anchor="middle">{String(r.k + 1).padStart(2, "0")}</text>
                  </g>
                ))}
                <circle cx="230" cy="220" r="66" class="core" />
                <text x="230" y="214" text-anchor="middle" class="core__t">1 processor</text>
                <text x="230" y="240" text-anchor="middle" class="core__s">2 FPS total</text>
                <g class="viewers rv" data-at="2">
                  {[0, 1, 2, 3].map((k) => <rect x={146 + k * 46} y="414" width="34" height="26" rx="5" />)}
                  <text x="230" y="404" text-anchor="middle">viewers → cache</text>
                </g>
              </svg>
            </Clip>
          </div>
          <aside class="notes" hidden>
            <p>The hand sweeps the cameras in turn: one budget, shared fairly. Missing sources are skipped, not waited on.</p>
            <p>This is the unit of scale: a city network is split into camera partitions, each with its own worker and the same scheduler. Viewers read cached output, so a control room of browsers costs no inference.</p>
          </aside>
        </section>

        <!-- MobileViT -->
        <section class="slide s-plain" data-builds="3" data-t="slide" aria-label="AI technique: plate OCR">
          <header class="hd"><p class="kicker">AI technique</p><span class="tag i">Architecture sketch</span></header>
          <h2>Transformer-style OCR, light enough to scale</h2>
          <div class="mvit">
            {[["Plate crop", "input"], ["Conv stem", "local edges"], ["MV2 blocks", "local features"], ["MobileViT-v2", "global layout"], ["Char head", "per position"]].map(([t, s], k) => (
              <div class:list={["mv rv", { hot: k === 3 }]} data-at="1" style={`transition-delay:${k * 0.12}s`}><b>{t}</b><span>{s}</span></div>
            ))}
            <div class="mv out rv" data-at="1" style="transition-delay:.6s"><code data-scramble="plate">KL07AB1234</code><span>+ confidences</span></div>
          </div>
          <div class="attn">
            <figure class="rv" data-at="2">
              <svg viewBox="0 0 300 150" aria-hidden="true" class="att quad">
                {Array.from({ length: 6 }, (_, a) => Array.from({ length: 6 }, (_, b) => <path d={`M${30 + a * 48} 30 L${30 + b * 48} 120`} />))}
                {Array.from({ length: 6 }, (_, a) => <><rect x={18 + a * 48} y="14" width="24" height="16" rx="3" /><rect x={18 + a * 48} y="120" width="24" height="16" rx="3" /></>)}
              </svg>
              <figcaption><b>Self-attention</b> every patch with every patch: cost grows with k²</figcaption>
            </figure>
            <figure class="rv" data-at="3">
              <svg viewBox="0 0 300 150" aria-hidden="true" class="att lin">
                {Array.from({ length: 6 }, (_, a) => <><path d={`M${30 + a * 48} 30 L150 75`} /><path d={`M150 75 L${30 + a * 48} 120`} /></>)}
                {Array.from({ length: 6 }, (_, a) => <><rect x={18 + a * 48} y="14" width="24" height="16" rx="3" /><rect x={18 + a * 48} y="120" width="24" height="16" rx="3" /></>)}
                <circle cx="150" cy="75" r="11" class="ctx" />
              </svg>
              <figcaption><b>Separable</b> patches meet one context vector: cost grows with k</figcaption>
            </figure>
            <div class="mvnote rv" data-at="3">
              <p>Pretrained (FastPlateOCR global model). We did not train it. Its accuracy on Indian plates is <b>unmeasured</b>.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <aside class="notes" hidden>
            <p>This is the AI core of the plate path. Convolutions capture local strokes; MobileViT-v2 blocks unfold the feature map into patches and use separable self-attention for global layout.</p>
            <p>Why it matters: separable attention is linear in patches, not quadratic, so it stays cheap per camera as the network grows. Be clear that it is pretrained and unvalidated on Indian plates.</p>
          </aside>
        </section>

        <!-- Tracking -->
        <section class="slide s-two wide" data-builds="3" data-t="slide" aria-label="Camera-local tracking">
          <div>
            <header class="hd"><p class="kicker">Tracking</p></header>
            <h2>Follow a car within one camera. Let go when unsure.</h2>
            <div class="costbox">
              <p class="eq"><code>cost = 0.75·dist + 0.20·(1 − IoU) + 0.05·Δarea</code></p>
              <div class="bars">
                <div class="bar rv" data-at="1"><span class="bar__fill" style="--w:0.75"></span><b>0.75</b><span>predicted centre distance</span></div>
                <div class="bar rv" data-at="1"><span class="bar__fill c2" style="--w:0.20"></span><b>0.20</b><span>box overlap, 1 − IoU</span></div>
                <div class="bar rv" data-at="1"><span class="bar__fill c3" style="--w:0.05"></span><b>0.05</b><span>change in box area</span></div>
              </div>
              <p class="note">Heuristic scores from <code>backend/tracking.py</code>, not identity probabilities.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="media">
            <Clip name="manim-tracking" label="Animation of camera-local tracking and ambiguity">
              <svg class="tracksvg" viewBox="0 0 520 440" aria-hidden="true">
                <rect x="0" y="0" width="520" height="440" rx="16" class="ts-bg" />
                <text x="20" y="34" class="ts-h">frame t → t+1 · minimum-cost assignment</text>
                <g class="rv" data-at="2">
                  <rect x="40" y="70" width="90" height="60" rx="6" class="ghost" /><text x="40" y="62" class="ts-l">track 4</text>
                  <rect x="120" y="96" width="90" height="60" rx="6" class="pred" />
                  <rect x="126" y="100" width="90" height="60" rx="6" class="det" />
                  <path d="M170 128 L171 130" class="link" />
                  <text x="230" y="134" class="ts-ok">matched · low cost</text>
                </g>
                <g class="rv" data-at="3">
                  <rect x="40" y="250" width="90" height="60" rx="6" class="ghost" /><text x="40" y="242" class="ts-l">track 7</text>
                  <rect x="40" y="350" width="90" height="60" rx="6" class="ghost" /><text x="40" y="428" class="ts-l">track 9</text>
                  <rect x="140" y="270" width="90" height="60" rx="6" class="pred" />
                  <rect x="140" y="332" width="90" height="60" rx="6" class="pred" />
                  <rect x="170" y="300" width="90" height="60" rx="6" class="det" />
                  <path d="M185 300 L215 330 M185 362 L215 330" class="link amb" />
                  <text x="280" y="318" class="ts-bad">two near-equal costs</text>
                  <text x="280" y="344" class="ts-bad">→ ambiguous, retire both</text>
                </g>
              </svg>
            </Clip>
          </div>
          <aside class="notes" hidden>
            <p>The cost is mostly distance from the predicted centre, then overlap, then area change. Class mismatch or a far jump rejects a pair outright.</p>
            <p>Click 3: when two tracks compete almost equally, Sakshi does not guess; those tracks retire. No appearance model, so no cross-camera identity.</p>
          </aside>
        </section>

        <!-- Evidence chain -->
        <section class="slide s-plain" data-builds="4" data-t="slide" aria-label="Evidence and audit">
